dialect grammar
grammar stdio.las.snapshot
extension las
start document

# Ticket 26/08/10/ARTIFACT-SYSTEM-OVERHAUL-REAL-CODECS-RUNTIME-REUSE-EVOLUTION: real
# `store::ArtifactDsl::print_dsl`/`parse_dsl` shape for `stdio.las`
# (../🦀️component.rs's handcrafted `impl store::ArtifactDsl for LasSnapshot`), replacing the old
# pseudo-ABNF placeholder this file used to contain (`%x`/single-quoted literals/`SP`/`NL`/bare
# `hex-pair`-as-a-hand-rolled-production -- all outside this framework's REAL `.grammar.semio`
# dialect: `dialect grammar\ngrammar <id>\nextension <ext>\nstart <production>`, one production per
# physical line, grouping always `{ }`, the built-in `hex` MACRO for an open-ended hex-digit run --
# per the ticket's own grammar-recipe doc §1, §3 pitfall #2). `print_dsl` is `wrap_text(envelope,
# body)` where `body` is `bytes.iter().map(|b| format!("{b:02x}")).collect()` over the REAL LAS
# binary buffer `engine::encode_las` produces -- a CONTINUOUS run of lowercase hex digit pairs, no
# separators (confirmed by direct read of `📸️snapshot/🦀️component.rs`'s `print_dsl`, not
# assumed). The framework's own `m5` conformance harness strips the mandatory
# `semio stdio.las.dsl v1` preamble line and substitutes the bare `envelope_id()` token
# (`"stdio.las"`) before calling `Recognizer::recognize` -- so `document` starts with that literal,
# not the full preamble line (matching json/csv/binary's own pilot precedent exactly). This leaf
# intentionally does NOT re-describe the byte-level LAS header/VLR/point-record structure that hex
# run decodes to -- the sibling `../💾️binary/📡️component.protocol.semio` file is the honest
# structural leaf (grammars never model binary, per png's own precedent this ticket's recipe cites
# for a binary-native artifact's text-facet grammar).
document = artifact-mark hex-body

artifact-mark = "stdio.las"

# P2-P1-fix note (mandatory, `p2-p1-fix-report.md`): an open-ended hex run MUST be the framework's
# built-in `hex` MACRO (bare `hex`, no matching production here -- `Symbol::Ref` falls back
# production-then-macro automatically), never a hand-rolled `{INT | IDENT}*`-shaped production --
# `Symbol::Star` is a single greedy, non-backtracking pass. `hex-body` has nothing following it to
# backtrack off of (it is the last token in the document), so this also sidesteps every
# hex/keyword-adjacency pitfall that macro was built for in the first place, and covers the
# empty-body case (`LasSnapshot::default()`, zero bytes) for free.
hex-body = hex
